WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.083 --> 00:00:14.843
The Mexican artist Frida Kahlo once said, "I suffered two grave accidents in my life, one in which a streetcar knocked me down. The other accident is Diego."

2
00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:22.470
For much of her life, pain tortured Kahlo's body, while her stormy marriage to the artist Diego Rivera tortured her heart.

3
00:00:22.471 --> 00:00:27.563
But the pain and heartbreak she suffered inspired her to create beauty from tragedy.

4
00:00:27.564 --> 00:00:36.474
When Frida Kahlo picked up a paintbrush, she used vibrant colors and unusual, powerful images to portray her struggles and deepest emotions.

5
00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:44.040
Frida Kahlo was born on July 6, 1907, in Coyoacán, a small town near Mexico City.

6
00:00:44.041 --> 00:00:55.127
Her father, Guillermo, was a photographer, and her mother, Matilde, was a deeply religious woman who took pride in caring for the family home, La Casa Azul (the Blue House).

7
00:00:55.128 --> 00:00:59.094
Of his six daughters, Frida was her father's favorite.

8
00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:03.371
When she was six years old, she was stricken by poliomyelitis.

9
00:01:03.372 --> 00:01:12.196
Encouraged by doctors to be active, she helped her adoring father in his studio, setting up cameras and lights and learning to develop pictures.

10
00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:16.196
Polio made Frida's right leg thinner and shorter than her left one.

11
00:01:16.197 --> 00:01:22.308
Despite her deformity, however, she was a happy, boyish girl who enjoyed boxing and biking.

12
00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:29.247
When Frida entered Mexico's National Preparatory School in 1922, she dreamed of becoming a doctor.

13
00:01:29.247 --> 00:01:33.072
At 15, Frida was bright, eager, and ambitious.

14
00:01:33.073 --> 00:01:40.398
Her striking looks—dark eyes, thick eyebrows—and outgoing personality made her popular among her classmates.

15
00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:48.715
Diego Rivera, a famous Mexican artist, was painting a mural at the school, and Frida skipped some of her classes to watch him work.

16
00:01:48.716 --> 00:01:53.889
Rivera's murals depicted Mexico's culture, industry, and political struggles.

17
00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:58.877
Even though the painter was twenty years older than Frida, she developed a crush on him.

18
00:01:58.878 --> 00:02:08.756
On September 17, 1925, Frida's life changed forever. She was riding a bus home from school when a streetcar crashed into it.

19
00:02:08.757 --> 00:02:19.327
The bus was torn in half, and Frida was badly hurt. Her spine and pelvis were fractured, her right leg and foot were broken, and a handrail pierced her abdomen.

20
00:02:19.328 --> 00:02:26.859
She would never be able to have a child. For months, Frida lay in bed, a plaster cast covering much of her body.

21
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:34.404
The cost of her medical care wiped out the family's savings, and her parents took a loan against La Casa Azul to pay the bills.

22
00:02:34.405 --> 00:02:41.452
Frustrated, bored, and in pain, Frida began painting to express her feelings and entertain herself.

23
00:02:41.453 --> 00:02:51.343
Her mother placed a mirror in the canopy of Frida's bed, and using a lap easel, Frida painted both self-portraits and portraits of friends who came to see her.

24
00:02:51.344 --> 00:03:00.828
By 1927, Frida had regained her ability to walk. Although she would always suffer physical pain, she returned to an active life.

25
00:03:00.829 --> 00:03:08.382
It was a time of political upheaval, and Frida joined others in calling for an end to the dictatorship controlling Mexico.

26
00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:16.987
She met writers, musicians, and artists who expressed similar political ideas in their work, and painting became Frida's passion.

27
00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:25.980
When she heard that Diego Rivera was doing a mural on the walls of a nearby government building, she visited him and asked him to look at her paintings.

28
00:03:25.981 --> 00:03:33.203
Rivera came to La Casa Azul and praised her work, beginning a relationship that would change both their lives.

29
00:03:33.204 --> 00:03:44.401
Diego and Frida were quickly attracted to each other. Rivera, 43, was charming and politically active, while Frida, 22, was a spirited, hopeful young painter.

30
00:03:44.402 --> 00:03:51.623
Her family did not approve of the match, and Frida's father even warned Diego that Frida would be sick all her life.

31
00:03:51.624 --> 00:03:55.972
Nevertheless, Frida married Diego in 1929.

32
00:03:55.973 --> 00:04:02.394
When Diego paid off the loan on La Casa Azul, his kindness only strengthened Frida's love for him.

33
00:04:02.395 --> 00:04:09.665
In 1930, Frida left Mexico for the first time when Diego was hired to paint murals in the United States.

34
00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:13.958
The couple traveled from San Francisco to New York and then Detroit.

35
00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:20.480
Frida often wore beautiful, traditional Mexican clothing and attracted attention wherever she went.

36
00:04:20.481 --> 00:04:25.448
Before long, she began to make friends and meet important art collectors.

37
00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:30.807
She continued to suffer complications from her accident, however, and she was homesick.

38
00:04:30.808 --> 00:04:41.350
Frida and Diego returned to Mexico in 1933 and built a new home, complete with side-by-side studios, but the next few years were turbulent.

39
00:04:41.351 --> 00:04:45.747
Frida faced long hospital stays as she underwent more surgery.

40
00:04:45.748 --> 00:04:52.184
Diego was unfaithful, and Frida began to have affairs too—their marriage was falling apart.

41
00:04:52.185 --> 00:04:59.414
Frida moved out of their new house and got her own apartment, but she missed Diego terribly and soon went back to him.

42
00:04:59.415 --> 00:05:08.225
By the late 1930s, Frida was gaining attention as an artist. In 1938, her paintings were shown in a New York City gallery.

43
00:05:08.226 --> 00:05:17.726
Critics praised her work, but the intensity of the paintings, some of which contained shocking images of blood and wounds, made many people uncomfortable.

44
00:05:17.727 --> 00:05:23.326
In 1939, Frida went to Europe, where her paintings were displayed in a Paris gallery.

45
00:05:23.327 --> 00:05:30.537
It was an exciting time for her, but when she returned to Mexico, she discovered that Diego was seeing yet another woman.

46
00:05:30.538 --> 00:05:38.870
Frida moved back to La Casa Azul, and she and Diego divorced; but despite their fights and heartaches, the couple decided to reconcile.

47
00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:46.409
They remarried in 1940, never to separate again. The next decade became the most creative period of Frida's life.

48
00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:53.845
During the 1940s, she created many vivid self-portraits, painting what she felt was the truth of her life.

49
00:05:53.846 --> 00:06:05.943
The lingering effects of the bus accident and her troubled marriage were both central subjects in her work, and she used symbols—images of animals, plants, buildings, and more—to express herself.

50
00:06:05.944 --> 00:06:12.403
She addressed the fears she had felt ever since her accident by painting her broken body and images of death.

51
00:06:12.404 --> 00:06:18.278
She conveyed both the love and insecurity she felt in her marriage with many portraits of Diego.

52
00:06:18.279 --> 00:06:25.062
By 1950, Frida had become a highly respected artist, but the pain in her body was increasing.

53
00:06:25.063 --> 00:06:32.907
She had been through more than thirty operations and endured another when her right leg was amputated below the knee because of gangrene.

54
00:06:32.908 --> 00:06:36.596
She returned to the comfort of La Casa Azul to recover.

55
00:06:36.597 --> 00:06:45.696
Despite her failing health, when Frida's paintings were displayed in Mexico City in 1953, she insisted on visiting the exhibit on opening night.

56
00:06:45.697 --> 00:06:54.099
Friends carried her bed to the gallery and placed her on it. Surrounded by her paintings, she enchanted the guests with songs and laughter.

57
00:06:54.100 --> 00:07:04.180
Frida Kahlo died at age 47 on July 13, 1954. She once said, "I never painted dreams. I painted my own reality."

58
00:07:04.181 --> 00:07:13.629
Her reality—which included immense physical and emotional pain—was one that many people could not imagine, but she expressed it in her work.

59
00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:18.240
Today, Frida has found her place in history as a world-famous artist.

60
00:07:18.241 --> 00:07:26.339
Every year, people from around the world travel to La Casa Azul, which is now a museum, so they can learn about Frida and her art.

61
00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:32.449
As they walk through the house, past Frida's wheelchairs and canes, they are reminded of her suffering.

62
00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:41.378
But her beautiful paintings also remind the visitors of a greater truth—that Frida Kahlo had the strength and courage to choose art over pain and sorrow.
